# Ralph progress: stamps-monetization

## Codebase Patterns

- Netlify Database migrations live in
  `netlify/database/migrations/<number>_<name>/migration.sql`.
- Reversible migrations can include a sibling `down.sql` rollback file.
- Schema tests use Vitest and read migration SQL as text from
  `netlify/database/migrations`.
- `tsconfig.json` includes `vitest/globals` because many existing tests use
  global Vitest APIs such as `expect`.
- Stamp accounting services use `getDatabase().pool.connect()` and explicit
  `BEGIN`/`COMMIT` transactions when multiple writes must stay consistent.
- Stamp debit should atomically update `stamp_lots` using a nested
  `SELECT ... FOR UPDATE SKIP LOCKED`, then update `users.stamps_balance`
  with a `stamps_balance > 0` guard in the same transaction.

## 2026-05-15 19:49 PDT - US-001
Thread: https://ampcode.com/threads/unknown
- Implemented the stamp accounting schema migration.
- Added `users.stamps_balance`, `stamp_lots`, and `stamp_transactions`.
- Added the FIFO consumption index and transaction history index.
- Added a `down.sql` rollback for the stamp tables and balance column.
- Files changed:
  - `netlify/database/migrations/0003_stamp_accounting/migration.sql`
  - `netlify/database/migrations/0003_stamp_accounting/down.sql`
  - `test/us001-stamp-schema.test.ts`
  - `tsconfig.json`
  - `specs/prd.json`
  - `progress.log`
- **Learnings for future iterations:**
  - The existing `users.id` column is `uuid`, so new stamp references need
    `uuid` foreign keys instead of the older text sketch in the design doc.
  - This repo already has text-based migration tests in Vitest; focused
    schema stories can follow that pattern without spinning up Postgres.
  - Rollback SQL is not present in older migrations, but stamp accounting
    uses a sibling `down.sql` to satisfy reversibility.
  - Typechecking the whole repo requires Vitest global types; existing tests
    use global `expect`.
